<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    houseCARL shared core (MCP-server step §8.2 — fork §6-B LOCKED: shared class library).
    The PROVEN read/write engines, extracted out of the dev harness so BOTH consumers call the
    SAME core: housecarl-generator (the byte-proof harness) and housecarl-mcp (the product server).
    Correctness transfers by construction — the harness's write-proof/read-proof oracles run
    against this exact code (see InternalsVisibleTo.cs).

    §8.2 moved the files VERBATIM ("move whole, tidy later" — Aaron 2026-06-01). §8.4 does the tidy:
    the namespace is now `HousecarlCore` (matches the assembly; the misleading `HousecarlGenerator`
    is gone), and the duplicated corpus loaders are consolidated onto `CorpusRulebook.Load`/`LoadCorpus`
    + the configurable `CorpusRulebook.CorpusPath` (the server, launched by MO2 from an arbitrary CWD,
    sets that path at startup — the old hardcoded "generated/corpus.json" couldn't survive the move).
    The clean (params)->(result) cleave lands per-tool as the server wires each one (the server is the
    real consumer that shapes the API); the proven cores it cleaves from already exist here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>HousecarlCore</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>housecarl-core</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The reflection target the extracted cores bind against. Pinned, matching both consumers; the pin moves for all three together on a bump. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Mutagen.Bethesda.Skyrim" Version="0.54.4" />
    <!-- The NIF layer's asset-internal reader/writer. Pure C#, GPL-3.0 (compatible with our GPL-3.0-only), source-
         generated from nifxml = coverage by construction.
         Pinned 1.1.0-housecarl.2 (2026-09-25, issue #926): houseCARL's own build of a fork, vendored in packages/local
         (nuget.config adds it). Fork: https://github.com/Avick3110/NiflySharp branch fix/bounded-counts, commit
         2fb5d048637f56b01d96369f03639d53539b5fe0, one commit on upstream ousnius/NiflySharp 7b55e88a (the commit Nifly
         1.1.0 was built from). The change: every list count, header count, string length and unknown-block size is
         checked against the bytes left before anything is allocated for it, and Load throws when a block reads past
         its stored size; each throws InvalidDataException naming the block and the number. On 1.1.0 one corrupted
         byte could make Load allocate 13 GB. Same assembly (NiflySharp 1.1.0.0) and the same public surface as 1.1.0.
         Built from a clean clone (git clone the fork, checkout the commit, git submodule update init for nifxml at
         292bb940; .NET 10 SDK) with: dotnet build NiflySharp/NiflySharp.csproj -c Release -p:Version=1.1.0-housecarl.2
         -p:ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true -p:RepositoryUrl=https://github.com/Avick3110/NiflySharp
         -p:PackageProjectUrl=https://github.com/Avick3110/NiflySharp
         The csproj's GeneratePackageOnBuild writes NiflySharp/bin/Release/Nifly.1.1.0-housecarl.2.nupkg; a bare dotnet
         pack fails NU5026 on a clean clone. ContinuousIntegrationBuild keeps the local path out of the DLLs, so two
         clones at different paths give byte-identical DLLs (and every package entry but the zip timestamps).
         housecarl.1 was the same commit built without it; bump the suffix on any repack, since a machine that restored
         a version keeps its cached copy.
         RE-PROVEN on bump, not assumed to carry over. 1.1.0 and the fork scanned back to back over the same 72,861 loose
         (every mod folder of the live instance) + 22,047 vanilla (Skyrim - Meshes0/1.bsa) meshes, loaded, round-tripped
         and diffed FILE BY FILE (spike-nif `scan` + diff_ab.py, ~24 min per loose leg): 0 files changed verdict, both
         legs 100% loaded clean. The #926 harness (random corruption of the authored test mesh): 137 + 38 runaways
         past 2 GB on 1.1.0 all come back as named errors, 0 runaways in 23,111 fork seeds (max 91 ms, 28 MB).
         Earlier: 1.0.0 to 1.1.0 (2026-07-26, #287) read 12 more loose meshes, same method. A percentage matching is NOT
         the check; two runs can hit the same totals while disagreeing about which files. Bump deliberately, and re-run
         that diff when you do.
         Notices: THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt (GPL-3.0 block). -->
    <PackageReference Include="Nifly" Version="1.1.0-housecarl.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- SkyPatcher grammar catalog (Wave 0b) — the CLOSED filter/op enumeration transcribed from the
         skypatcher-authoring reference. Embedded so SkyPatcherCatalog.Load() is self-contained (no beside-
         the-exe path), like the record corpus is generated by construction. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="skypatcher-catalog.json" />
    <!-- SkyPatcher op → Mutagen field map (Wave 1) — hand-modeled like the catalog, machine-verified by the
         fieldmap guard (every path walked with the real write engine; every valueMap target parsed against
         the real leaf enum). -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="skypatcher-fieldmap.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
